he idea of a  gaming loop is where a player is  presented with a challenge it's in every  game a player is presented with a  challenge they have a tool set to solve  that challenge then they beat it or they  don't beat it and they learn something  and ideally they get rewarded  for it it's this continual loop that  happens moment to moment in games it  also happens between matches so a great  example of that is titanfall where you  spawn in you fight the other team you do  great you get your Titan you do more  great work with your Titan and hopefully  you win the match battlefield 5 doesn't  really have that loop because of the  smaller map sizes and the random vehicle  spawns there's no reward for you doing  anything in my opinion anymore yeah  there's the squad commander system but  that's one person in your squad who's  like getting rewarded so basically you  spawn you run somewhere you die you  spawn you run somewhere you die you you  do it over and over again and you never  feel like you're getting your Titan to  drop right so like the tanks are just  sitting in the back of the field and you  have to randomly die at a time when a  tank is randomly spawning and then  randomly hope you have a good round in  that tank and not get killed by an  engineer I think that World War 3 in a  nutshell is doing a much better job at  this loop so they have battle points you  spend those battle points on the tank or  like on the artillery strike or on the  other elements of the game almost like a  little bit of a Call of Duty element to  it it's very similar to the Call of  Duty's scorestreak system but it's not  as aggressively dependent on whether  you're kicking a whole bunch of ass the  game also improves on baltha forward in  certain ways that are really interesting  and moves that the genre forward a bit  like you can choose to get bonus points  for attacking or defending objectives so  you can sit back and choose to defend  and get rewarded for it or you can  choose to 